# Project-local clangd config (overrides the user's global ~/.config/clangd).
#
# Use whatever g++ is active on PATH — inside this project's `nix develop`
# dev shell that resolves to the project's own flake gcc (libstdc++), which is
# exactly what build.sh compiles with. This avoids depending on a hardcoded
# nix-store path baked into the global dotfiles config (which points at a
# *different* gcc and can be garbage-collected).
#
# Notes:
#   - Launch your editor from inside the dev shell (e.g. via direnv) so `g++`
#     resolves to the nix toolchain, not /usr/bin/g++ (Apple Clang).
#   - macOS caveat: the nix `clang-tools` clangd injects a second libc++ and
#     cannot cleanly parse gcc's libstdc++ — on macOS point your editor at
#     Apple's clangd (/usr/bin/clangd). This file does not control which clangd
#     binary runs; that lives in your editor's LSP config.
CompileFlags:
  Compiler: g++
